[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to Change Streams in MongoDB"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Change Streams provide a real-time data stream of changes occurring within your MongoDB deployment. They allow applications to react immediately to data modifications, enabling powerful features like real-time analytics, data synchronization, and event-driven architectures."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Change Events: Represent individual modifications to the database, such as inserts, updates, deletes, and replacements."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Resume Token: An opaque token that identifies a specific point in the change stream. It allows you to resume the stream from where it left off, even after a disconnection."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Full Document Lookup: For update and replace events, you can configure the change stream to return the entire document that was modified."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Enabling Change Streams"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Change Streams are enabled by default on MongoDB replica sets and sharded clusters. No specific configuration is typically required to start using them."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Opening a Change Stream"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can open a change stream on a single collection, a database, or an entire MongoDB deployment. The following examples demonstrate how to open a change stream using the MongoDB shell and a programming language (Python)."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Using MongoDB Shell"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Open a change stream on a collection:
db.collection('myCollection').watch()

// Open a change stream on a database:
db.watch()

// Open a change stream on the entire deployment:
Mongo().watch()"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Python (PyMongo)"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"from pymongo import MongoClient

client = MongoClient('mongodb://localhost:27017/')
db = client['mydatabase']
collection = db['mycollection']

with collection.watch() as change_stream:
    for change in change_stream:
        print(change)"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Change Stream Output"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Each change event returned by the change stream is a document containing information about the modification. The structure of the document varies depending on the type of event."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"{'_id': {'_data': '...'}, 'operationType': 'insert', 'ns': {'db': 'mydatabase', 'coll': 'mycollection'}, 'documentKey': {'_id': ObjectId('...')}, 'fullDocument': {'_id': ObjectId('...'), 'name': 'New Document'}}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Key fields in the change event document include: '_id' (the resume token), 'operationType' (the type of operation), 'ns' (the namespace), 'documentKey' (the identifier of the modified document), and 'fullDocument' (the complete document after the modification, if requested)."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Filtering Change Events"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can filter the change events returned by the stream using aggregation pipelines. This allows you to focus on specific types of changes or changes to particular documents."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"pipeline = [{
    '$match': {
        'operationType': 'update',
        'updateDescription.updatedFields.status': 'completed'
    }
}]

with collection.watch(pipeline=pipeline) as change_stream:
    for change in change_stream:
        print(change)"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example filters for 'update' operations where the 'status' field is updated to 'completed'."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Resuming Change Streams"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"If a change stream is interrupted, you can resume it from the last processed event using the resume token. Store the '_id' field from the last processed change event and pass it to the 'resume_after' option when opening a new change stream."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"resume_token = ... # Your stored resume token

with collection.watch(resume_after=resume_token) as change_stream:
    for change in change_stream:
        print(change)"},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Use Cases"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Real-time Analytics: Update dashboards and reports instantly as data changes."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Synchronization: Keep multiple systems in sync by replicating changes in real-time."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Event-Driven Architectures: Trigger actions and notifications based on specific data modifications."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Change Streams provide a powerful mechanism for building real-time applications with MongoDB. By understanding the key concepts and techniques outlined in this introduction, you can leverage Change Streams to create responsive and data-driven solutions."}]
